/-! ## Vector theorems — each is a mutation killer

Generated by a python3 reference (cross-checked
against BIP144 §Specification and Core @cce32b168
UnserializeTransaction:212-239). Any single-byte edit to the codec's
framing, stack order, superfluous ban, or flag ban flips at least one
of these from true to false.

* V1/K1,K5: empty-witness tx takes the OLD format; decode gives
  witness = [] and `encodeTx144 tx1 = v1 = serTx tx1`.
* V2/K1,K2: marker 0x00 + flag 0x01 framing; one stack of two items
  (20 B + 71 B, P2WPKH shape) for the single input.
* V3/K2: two inputs ⇒ two stacks in input order; the SECOND stack
  empty and encoded as a single 0x00 byte (dropping or reordering
  empty stacks breaks v3_decode).
* V4/K1: coinbase-shaped null prevout under the framing.
* V5/K4: flag 0x02 (bit 0 clear, residual bit) ⇒ `unknownFlag`.
* V6/K4: flag 0x03 (witness path taken, residual bits) ⇒ `unknownFlag`
  AFTER the stacks — Core raises "Unknown transaction optional data".
* V7/K3: flag 0x01 with a single all-empty stack ⇒ `superfluousWitness`
  (Core "Superfluous witness record"); the same tx without the framing
  is exactly v1 and decodes fine.
* V8/K1: marker 0x00 AND flag 0x00 ⇒ NO vin/vout re-read (Core reads
  the dummy as an empty vin and skips straight to locktime); the codec
  yields the 0-in/0-out tx that INV-001 rejects end-to-end. -/

/-! ### Shared leaf facts (cheap kernel `rfl`s at literal offsets)

One `rfl` per `rdBytes`/`rdCs`/`rdFlag`/`isEmpty` call site, stated at
concrete offsets so the kernel never runs the expensive computed-offset
`ByteArray.extract` loop over a whole transaction at once. -/

